## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical replay-protection patterns in multi-tenant SSO architectures, along with real-world token normalization from OIDC and SAML protocols, JWKS key rotation, and designing scalable validation endpoints.

## Key Concepts

- Token validation across OIDC and SAML standards
- Per-tenant JWKS rotation and SAML metadata caching
- Nonce-based replay protection with time-to-live (TTL) enforcement
- Internal JWT issuance including tenant_id, user_id, scopes, and nonce

## Code Example

```javascript
// Issue internal token with nonce (Node.js)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

async function issueInternalToken({ tenant_id, user_id, scopes, nonce }) {
  const payload = {
    tenant_id,
    sub: user_id,
    scopes,
    nonce,
    iat: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000),
    exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) + 300 // 5-minute expiry
  };
  return jwt.sign(payload, getInternalSigningKey(tenant_id), { algorithm: 'RS256' });
}

// Validate nonce during token validation
async function validateAndConsumeNonce(tenant_id, nonce) {
  const key = `nonce:${tenant_id}:${nonce}`;
  const exists = await redis.setnx(key, '1');
  if (!exists) {
    throw new Error('Replay detected: nonce already used');
  }
  await redis.expire(key, 300); // Match token TTL
  return true;
}

// /validate endpoint - lightweight claim extraction
app.post('/validate', async (req, res) => {
  const { token } = req.body;
  const [header] = token.split('.');
  const decodedHeader = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(header, 'base64'));
  const tenant_id = decodedHeader.tenant_id;

  const claims = await extractClaimsWithoutFullDecode(token, tenant_id);
  return res.json({ valid: true, claims });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cache invalidation for multi-tenant scales with Redis as the source of truth and Memcached as a secondary layer. Requires concrete event schemas, per-tenant isolation, and practical guards against stale reads.

## Key Concepts

- Cache-invalidation workflow across Redis and Memcached with tenant isolation
- Event schema design (tenantId, articleId, version) and idempotent handling
- Stampede protection (hot-key guards, probabilistic refresh) and recovery strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Redis publish invalidation event
function publishInvalidation(tenantId, articleId, newVersion) {
  const event = { tenantId, articleId, version: newVersion, timestamp: Date.now() };
  redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify(event));
}

// Redis listener and Memcached worker
function handleInvalidation(event) {
  const key = `article:${event.tenantId}:${event.articleId}`;
  redis.del(key);
  redis.incr(`version:${event.tenantId}`);

  // Memcached CAS invalidation
  memcached.gets(key, (err, data, cas) => {
    if (data) memcached.cas(key, '', cas, 0, () => {});
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a scalable, low-latency per-tenant policy engine integrated with token validation across OIDC and SAML protocols.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy evaluation and enforcement
- Token routing (OIDC vs SAML)
- Cache strategy with TTL management
- Policy versioning and hot reload
- OpenAPI security alignment

## Code Example
```javascript
// Middleware validates token type, applies OIDC or SAML validation,
// fetches tenant policy, enforces claims/audience/rate limits before routing
app.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const policy = await policyStore.get(req.tenantId);
  const validation = req.isSaml ? validateSAML : validateOIDC;
  if (!(await validation(req.token, policy))) {
    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Policy violation' });
  }
  await enforceRateLimit(req, policy);
  next();
});
